WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.982
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:09.187 --> 00:00:10.270
FORREST BRAZEAL: Well, hi.

00:00:10.270 --> 00:00:11.200
I'm Forrest Brazeal.

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:12.610
I'm a Cloud builder
and educator.

00:00:12.610 --> 00:00:14.950
And I work here at Google Cloud.

00:00:14.950 --> 00:00:17.770
Over the years, as I talk to
members of the Cloud community,

00:00:17.770 --> 00:00:20.650
I constantly get asked versions
of the same question, which

00:00:20.650 --> 00:00:23.590
is, how do I get into a
Cloud or DevOps engineering

00:00:23.590 --> 00:00:27.070
role from what we might call
a traditional IT career?

00:00:27.070 --> 00:00:29.433
So you might be a Windows
Server admin or a DBA.

00:00:29.433 --> 00:00:31.600
You might be racking and
stacking servers or working

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:33.130
in a NOC, but you're
doing all that

00:00:33.130 --> 00:00:36.190
in the context of a traditional
data center, not in the Cloud.

00:00:36.190 --> 00:00:37.780
I actually started
my career that way

00:00:37.780 --> 00:00:39.405
as well doing many
of the things I just

00:00:39.405 --> 00:00:42.620
mentioned before moving into a
cloud role several years ago.

00:00:42.620 --> 00:00:44.470
And in this video,
we're going to lay out

00:00:44.470 --> 00:00:47.710
a path for how you can make
that transition from a legacy IT

00:00:47.710 --> 00:00:50.600
career to a role where you
get to build on Google Cloud

00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:51.640
Services.

00:00:51.640 --> 00:00:52.700
So let's dive right in.

00:00:52.700 --> 00:00:53.200
OK.

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:55.210
The first question you're going
to want to ask yourself is why.

00:00:55.210 --> 00:00:58.030
What's the point of choosing a
Cloud role when maybe I already

00:00:58.030 --> 00:00:59.620
have a career that's going fine.

00:00:59.620 --> 00:01:00.580
I'm in a good groove.

00:01:00.580 --> 00:01:02.500
And an easy answer might
be the Cloud market

00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:03.513
is very hot right now.

00:01:03.513 --> 00:01:04.930
Take a look at
these numbers here.

00:01:04.930 --> 00:01:07.900
Look at these salaries
associated with Google Cloud

00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:08.525
certifications.

00:01:08.525 --> 00:01:09.983
This data is from
Global Knowledge.

00:01:09.983 --> 00:01:11.800
And to be clear, I
didn't cherry pick this.

00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:14.380
Those top two numbers you see
there for Google Cloud certs,

00:01:14.380 --> 00:01:16.922
those aren't just the top two
paying Google Cloud certs.

00:01:16.922 --> 00:01:18.880
They aren't just the top
two paying cloud certs

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:19.450
in the world.

00:01:19.450 --> 00:01:23.410
Those are the top two certs
anywhere in IT as of 2021.

00:01:23.410 --> 00:01:26.860
Just to be very clear, you know
and I know that certifications

00:01:26.860 --> 00:01:28.750
are not the whole story here.

00:01:28.750 --> 00:01:31.030
It's very difficult to get
hired into a cloud role

00:01:31.030 --> 00:01:33.520
unless you can show some
hands-on experience working

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:35.680
with cloud, even
if you've already

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:37.970
got experience working
in a traditional IT role.

00:01:37.970 --> 00:01:40.450
So how are you going
to get that experience?

00:01:40.450 --> 00:01:42.430
And this is kind of a
unique problem for you,

00:01:42.430 --> 00:01:45.220
because you may not be able
to just go back to square one

00:01:45.220 --> 00:01:47.683
and start again with schooling
or something like that.

00:01:47.683 --> 00:01:48.850
You've already got a career.

00:01:48.850 --> 00:01:49.933
Maybe you've got a family.

00:01:49.933 --> 00:01:52.570
You can't take two steps back
to take one step forward.

00:01:52.570 --> 00:01:54.940
You've got to find a path
to this cloud role that's

00:01:54.940 --> 00:01:57.910
going to enable you to augment
your skills without starting

00:01:57.910 --> 00:01:58.420
over.

00:01:58.420 --> 00:02:00.100
So here's what I
recommend that you do.

00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:03.820
We're going to cover three steps
to your cloud career coming out

00:02:03.820 --> 00:02:04.840
of traditional IT.

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:07.460
We might call them the
three IT cloud commandments.

00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:09.310
The first one is that
you need to exploit

00:02:09.310 --> 00:02:11.200
your unfair advantage.

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:12.260
What do I mean by this?

00:02:12.260 --> 00:02:14.230
You'll hear this term used
a lot in the startup world

00:02:14.230 --> 00:02:16.150
where founders talk about
finding the one thing they

00:02:16.150 --> 00:02:18.280
do better than anybody else
and just really doubling down

00:02:18.280 --> 00:02:18.890
on that.

00:02:18.890 --> 00:02:20.960
Well, if you're
watching this video,

00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:22.960
your unfair advantage may
be that you're already

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:24.610
employed in some form of tech.

00:02:24.610 --> 00:02:27.010
IT is in your blood a
little bit as you were.

00:02:27.010 --> 00:02:29.135
And that's going to give
you a tremendous advantage

00:02:29.135 --> 00:02:31.135
relative to other people
who are trying to break

00:02:31.135 --> 00:02:32.410
into cloud for the first time.

00:02:32.410 --> 00:02:34.280
But you've got to
access this advantage.

00:02:34.280 --> 00:02:36.880
And that's going to likely
look taking advantage

00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:38.440
of the existing
opportunities you

00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:41.697
have to get close to cloud
in your professional context.

00:02:41.697 --> 00:02:43.780
Cloud builds on fundamentals
that have been around

00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:44.920
for a long time--

00:02:44.920 --> 00:02:48.620
compute, networking, storage,
all forms of access control.

00:02:48.620 --> 00:02:50.620
These are things that you
understand that you've

00:02:50.620 --> 00:02:51.940
got some context with.

00:02:51.940 --> 00:02:55.540
So why not figure out how to
cloudify those existing skills,

00:02:55.540 --> 00:02:58.720
how to turn those skills into
cloud versions of themselves?

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:00.680
There's three steps
to doing this.

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:02.530
The first step is simply
to build the thing

00:03:02.530 --> 00:03:03.740
that you already know.

00:03:03.740 --> 00:03:06.820
So let's say that you're a SQL
Server DBA, like I used to be.

00:03:06.820 --> 00:03:07.360
OK.

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:08.818
Well, let's see if
you can stand up

00:03:08.818 --> 00:03:10.335
a SQL Server in Google Cloud.

00:03:10.335 --> 00:03:11.710
You can use the
console for this.

00:03:11.710 --> 00:03:13.060
You can point and click around.

00:03:13.060 --> 00:03:16.120
The point is just to transfer
your knowledge of standing up

00:03:16.120 --> 00:03:19.540
a SQL Server on the old data
center in the virtual machine

00:03:19.540 --> 00:03:21.490
to standing it up
in Google Cloud.

00:03:21.490 --> 00:03:22.240
Second step.

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:25.280
Once you've done that, go ahead
and automate that same process.

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:27.490
So now instead of pointing
and clicking, instead of

00:03:27.490 --> 00:03:31.027
using click ops, you're going
to use some form of automation.

00:03:31.027 --> 00:03:32.860
You're going to use
something like Terraform

00:03:32.860 --> 00:03:35.660
to automate the standing up
of that underlying server.

00:03:35.660 --> 00:03:37.660
You're going to use some
scripting or automation

00:03:37.660 --> 00:03:39.910
to make sure that that
SQL Server is initialized

00:03:39.910 --> 00:03:41.140
the same way every time.

00:03:41.140 --> 00:03:43.210
You're going to use some
form of source control

00:03:43.210 --> 00:03:45.910
to make sure that you keep
track of all that configuration.

00:03:45.910 --> 00:03:48.288
And you're going to use some
sort of a CI/CD pipeline,

00:03:48.288 --> 00:03:50.080
we assume, to make sure
that that code gets

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:52.360
from your laptop into
the cloud the same way

00:03:52.360 --> 00:03:54.373
every time without you
touching it in between.

00:03:54.373 --> 00:03:56.290
And then once you've
done that, the third step

00:03:56.290 --> 00:03:59.000
is to try to reproduce the
same idea using Managed Cloud

00:03:59.000 --> 00:03:59.915
Services.

00:03:59.915 --> 00:04:01.790
So instead of SQL Server,
you might say, hey,

00:04:01.790 --> 00:04:04.570
I wonder if I could achieve the
same results using Google Cloud

00:04:04.570 --> 00:04:06.460
SQL or maybe Google
Cloud Spanner.

00:04:06.460 --> 00:04:07.365
Give it a try.

00:04:07.365 --> 00:04:09.490
It's going to expand the
circle of what you already

00:04:09.490 --> 00:04:11.283
know to encompass the cloud.

00:04:11.283 --> 00:04:13.450
Now, on the other hand, you
may see things happening

00:04:13.450 --> 00:04:15.820
in your company that are
cloud related that you are not

00:04:15.820 --> 00:04:17.680
part of and don't feel
like you have the ability

00:04:17.680 --> 00:04:18.519
to access right now.

00:04:18.519 --> 00:04:20.394
There might be a cloud
engineering department

00:04:20.394 --> 00:04:21.670
over there across the hall.

00:04:21.670 --> 00:04:22.540
And you see what they're doing.

00:04:22.540 --> 00:04:24.332
And it looks really
cool, but it's just not

00:04:24.332 --> 00:04:25.930
related to your
current job at all.

00:04:25.930 --> 00:04:28.328
In that case, I would recommend
go talk to those people.

00:04:28.328 --> 00:04:30.370
See if you can sit down
and have lunch with them.

00:04:30.370 --> 00:04:31.390
Ask to pick their brain.

00:04:31.390 --> 00:04:32.830
Ask for some mentorship.

00:04:32.830 --> 00:04:33.610
Buy them coffee.

00:04:33.610 --> 00:04:36.160
Max out your access
to colleagues who

00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:37.718
do what you would like to do.

00:04:37.718 --> 00:04:39.760
That's part of your unfair
advantage right now is

00:04:39.760 --> 00:04:42.220
having access to those
people and to their problems.

00:04:42.220 --> 00:04:43.062
Who knows?

00:04:43.062 --> 00:04:45.520
Worst case, you're going to
learn a lot about what they do.

00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:47.440
You're going to get great
advice from them on where you

00:04:47.440 --> 00:04:48.762
need to focus your skilling up.

00:04:48.762 --> 00:04:51.220
Best case, they might have an
internal role opening up soon

00:04:51.220 --> 00:04:52.670
that you are top of mind for.

00:04:52.670 --> 00:04:54.670
Just leverage the heck
out of those connections.

00:04:54.670 --> 00:04:56.212
Only good things
are going to happen.

00:04:56.212 --> 00:04:58.960
But-- and this is important--
if those opportunities aren't

00:04:58.960 --> 00:05:01.180
coming to you
naturally, you don't

00:05:01.180 --> 00:05:03.580
need to sit around
forever and wait for them.

00:05:03.580 --> 00:05:05.503
Ultimately, it's
not your team that's

00:05:05.503 --> 00:05:07.420
responsible for the
trajectory of your career.

00:05:07.420 --> 00:05:08.500
It's not your employer.

00:05:08.500 --> 00:05:10.660
It is you who's
responsible for it.

00:05:10.660 --> 00:05:13.350
So if the cloud opportunities
are not coming to you,

00:05:13.350 --> 00:05:15.070
you need to go out and get them.

00:05:15.070 --> 00:05:18.070
And that brings us to our
second IT to Cloud commandment.

00:05:18.070 --> 00:05:20.930
And the second commandment
is to boost targeted skills.

00:05:20.930 --> 00:05:23.430
And I'm being very deliberate
in saying it that way because,

00:05:23.430 --> 00:05:24.810
again, as we've
said, you may not

00:05:24.810 --> 00:05:26.477
have time to kind of
burn the house down

00:05:26.477 --> 00:05:29.580
and start from square one
with going back to school

00:05:29.580 --> 00:05:31.305
and building up every
skill from scratch.

00:05:31.305 --> 00:05:33.180
You've got to figure
out where your gaps are.

00:05:33.180 --> 00:05:34.980
And you've got to
make very intentional

00:05:34.980 --> 00:05:37.170
targeted improvements
to those gaps.

00:05:37.170 --> 00:05:39.910
There are four areas that
you can dive into here.

00:05:39.910 --> 00:05:42.855
And the first is you
have to learn to code.

00:05:42.855 --> 00:05:45.480
There's a reason that people who
come from software development

00:05:45.480 --> 00:05:48.210
backgrounds tend to find it
much easier to get in the Cloud

00:05:48.210 --> 00:05:50.640
than people who come from
traditional IT backgrounds.

00:05:50.640 --> 00:05:54.270
And that is because code is
a non-negotiable if you want

00:05:54.270 --> 00:05:55.530
to become a cloud engineer.

00:05:55.530 --> 00:05:57.780
You're not able to just point
and click or use the GUI

00:05:57.780 --> 00:05:58.980
to manage things anymore.

00:05:58.980 --> 00:06:00.390
You have to be able
to automate if you're

00:06:00.390 --> 00:06:02.100
going to keep up with the
massive scale of a cloud

00:06:02.100 --> 00:06:02.830
environment.

00:06:02.830 --> 00:06:04.320
So if you don't have a cloud--

00:06:04.320 --> 00:06:06.150
if you don't have a language
you already know, try Python.

00:06:06.150 --> 00:06:08.025
It's pretty ubiquitous
in cloud environments.

00:06:08.025 --> 00:06:09.150
But code is the barrier.

00:06:09.150 --> 00:06:12.060
And code is the bridge to
a cloud engineering role.

00:06:12.060 --> 00:06:14.730
You don't necessarily have
to be an elite code person.

00:06:14.730 --> 00:06:16.548
You don't have to be
crunching algorithms.

00:06:16.548 --> 00:06:18.840
That's not going to come up
a lot in a cloud interview.

00:06:18.840 --> 00:06:21.180
But you do need to be able
to perform basic automation

00:06:21.180 --> 00:06:24.750
and data manipulation tasks that
do come up every day in a cloud

00:06:24.750 --> 00:06:25.380
role.

00:06:25.380 --> 00:06:26.280
Learn to code.

00:06:26.280 --> 00:06:27.000
Second thing.

00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:28.860
Learn the basics of
Linux and networking.

00:06:28.860 --> 00:06:30.660
And this may be
something that feels

00:06:30.660 --> 00:06:33.460
more core to what you've been
doing in IT and that's great.

00:06:33.460 --> 00:06:35.940
But you will need to
remember the Linux file

00:06:35.940 --> 00:06:37.148
system, basic Linux commands.

00:06:37.148 --> 00:06:39.482
You'll want to make sure you
understand the fundamentals

00:06:39.482 --> 00:06:40.410
of containerization.

00:06:40.410 --> 00:06:43.770
And of course, you'll want to
the core networking concepts

00:06:43.770 --> 00:06:45.930
like the IP Protocol and
all the other protocols

00:06:45.930 --> 00:06:49.460
that layer on top of it, DNS,
subnets, all that good stuff.

00:06:49.460 --> 00:06:51.460
Again, some of this may
be more familiar to you.

00:06:51.460 --> 00:06:52.502
You've touched it before.

00:06:52.502 --> 00:06:54.540
There's lots of
courses and videos

00:06:54.540 --> 00:06:56.110
out there you can
use as a refresher.

00:06:56.110 --> 00:06:58.410
But these are not going
away in the cloud.

00:06:58.410 --> 00:06:59.910
They're only becoming
more important

00:06:59.910 --> 00:07:01.950
because so many systems
are built on top of them.

00:07:01.950 --> 00:07:03.810
And you will want to make sure
that you're fresh on those

00:07:03.810 --> 00:07:05.060
when you go into an interview.

00:07:05.060 --> 00:07:06.960
So code, Linux, and networking.

00:07:06.960 --> 00:07:07.710
Third thing.

00:07:07.710 --> 00:07:09.430
If you want to get a cloud
job, believe it or not,

00:07:09.430 --> 00:07:10.590
you actually should
know a little bit

00:07:10.590 --> 00:07:11.590
about the cloud itself.

00:07:11.590 --> 00:07:13.590
In this video, we're
talking about Google Cloud.

00:07:13.590 --> 00:07:15.240
So how are you going to
learn about Google Cloud?

00:07:15.240 --> 00:07:17.070
I recommend Google
Cloud Skills Boost.

00:07:17.070 --> 00:07:20.200
It's Google's own platform for
training you on Google Cloud.

00:07:20.200 --> 00:07:22.530
And you can check out the
infrastructure modernization

00:07:22.530 --> 00:07:23.350
track there.

00:07:23.350 --> 00:07:25.350
It's got a nice combination
of hands-on learning

00:07:25.350 --> 00:07:26.500
and certification prep.

00:07:26.500 --> 00:07:27.000
Yeah.

00:07:27.000 --> 00:07:28.710
We talked about how
certifications maybe

00:07:28.710 --> 00:07:30.930
have some limitations, but I
do think it's important for you

00:07:30.930 --> 00:07:32.460
to have a Google
Cloud certification

00:07:32.460 --> 00:07:34.860
if you're trying to transition
into a Google Cloud role.

00:07:34.860 --> 00:07:36.695
And given where you
are in your career,

00:07:36.695 --> 00:07:38.820
I would recommend you go
after a professional level

00:07:38.820 --> 00:07:41.940
certification like the Google
Cloud Professional Architect.

00:07:41.940 --> 00:07:43.500
It's going to be
difficult, but it's

00:07:43.500 --> 00:07:45.030
going to signal that
you're serious, that you're

00:07:45.030 --> 00:07:46.050
a pro on this subject.

00:07:46.050 --> 00:07:47.925
And given where you are,
I think that's going

00:07:47.925 --> 00:07:49.320
to be necessary for you.

00:07:49.320 --> 00:07:49.920
Code.

00:07:49.920 --> 00:07:51.120
Linux and networking.

00:07:51.120 --> 00:07:52.237
Go deep on Google Cloud.

00:07:52.237 --> 00:07:53.820
And then finally,
you're going to want

00:07:53.820 --> 00:07:56.490
to have some familiarity
with open source tooling.

00:07:56.490 --> 00:07:58.470
Whatever cloud provider
you go to work on,

00:07:58.470 --> 00:08:00.053
there's going to be
certain tools that

00:08:00.053 --> 00:08:01.350
pop up in almost every shop.

00:08:01.350 --> 00:08:02.065
Doing automation?

00:08:02.065 --> 00:08:02.940
I've already said it.

00:08:02.940 --> 00:08:05.010
Terraform is probably going
to be involved in some way.

00:08:05.010 --> 00:08:05.940
Working with containers?

00:08:05.940 --> 00:08:07.940
Kubernetes is probably
going to be on the table.

00:08:07.940 --> 00:08:08.880
These are open source.

00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:09.450
They travel.

00:08:09.450 --> 00:08:10.840
They're portable between clouds.

00:08:10.840 --> 00:08:12.090
Take some time to learn those.

00:08:12.090 --> 00:08:13.840
Use something like
Kubernetes the Hard Way

00:08:13.840 --> 00:08:14.910
from Kelsey Hightower.

00:08:14.910 --> 00:08:17.070
Figure out what Kubernetes
is, how it works,

00:08:17.070 --> 00:08:19.020
what problems it's
good for solving.

00:08:19.020 --> 00:08:21.720
With Terraform, learn how to
deploy some cloud services.

00:08:21.720 --> 00:08:24.870
And this brings us to a piece of
advice that should tie together

00:08:24.870 --> 00:08:26.650
all four of the areas
I just mentioned,

00:08:26.650 --> 00:08:28.410
which is before you
start interviewing,

00:08:28.410 --> 00:08:31.410
you'll want to build a
couple of hands-on projects.

00:08:31.410 --> 00:08:32.530
What do I mean by that?

00:08:32.530 --> 00:08:34.169
Take these cloud
skills you've learned

00:08:34.169 --> 00:08:36.840
and actually create something
that's real, that's live,

00:08:36.840 --> 00:08:39.270
that's out there in the world,
that you can use as a story

00:08:39.270 --> 00:08:40.799
during an interview.

00:08:40.799 --> 00:08:41.966
I'm very serious about this.

00:08:41.966 --> 00:08:43.632
For a couple of years,
I've been running

00:08:43.632 --> 00:08:46.050
a community initiative called
the Cloud Resume Challenge.

00:08:46.050 --> 00:08:46.740
It's free.

00:08:46.740 --> 00:08:48.087
You can do it on Google Cloud.

00:08:48.087 --> 00:08:50.670
And it'll get you up close and
personal with almost everything

00:08:50.670 --> 00:08:51.480
I just mentioned.

00:08:51.480 --> 00:08:53.938
Give you something that you
can talk about in an interview.

00:08:53.938 --> 00:08:57.130
You can check that out at
Cloudresumechallenge.dev.

00:08:57.130 --> 00:08:57.630
OK.

00:08:57.630 --> 00:08:59.880
So let's say you've exploited
your unfair advantage as

00:08:59.880 --> 00:09:00.697
much as you can.

00:09:00.697 --> 00:09:02.340
You found your skills gaps.

00:09:02.340 --> 00:09:03.970
You've plugged them
as much as you can.

00:09:03.970 --> 00:09:05.100
And now here you
are in interviews.

00:09:05.100 --> 00:09:07.680
And you're still not getting
traction on that cloud engineer

00:09:07.680 --> 00:09:09.690
or cloud architect
role that you wanted.

00:09:09.690 --> 00:09:10.240
Don't worry.

00:09:10.240 --> 00:09:11.820
This brings us to
the third of our IT

00:09:11.820 --> 00:09:14.160
to Cloud commandments,
which is you may need

00:09:14.160 --> 00:09:17.820
to take a stepping stone role,
not moving backward but taking

00:09:17.820 --> 00:09:21.660
an intermediate step before you
get to your end destination.

00:09:21.660 --> 00:09:23.160
And there's a lot
of different roles

00:09:23.160 --> 00:09:25.320
you can take that'll move
you closer to the cloud.

00:09:25.320 --> 00:09:27.180
I'm not going to be exhaustive
here on what I mention,

00:09:27.180 --> 00:09:29.013
but I'm going to list
several that I've seen

00:09:29.013 --> 00:09:30.480
be helpful to a lot of people.

00:09:30.480 --> 00:09:32.730
Number one-- and this is the
first thing I would try--

00:09:32.730 --> 00:09:35.230
you can look for an IT admin
role that has a little bit more

00:09:35.230 --> 00:09:36.790
of a hybrid aspect to it.

00:09:36.790 --> 00:09:39.330
So while cloud
qualifications are not a must

00:09:39.330 --> 00:09:40.970
in order for you
to get hired, you

00:09:40.970 --> 00:09:43.020
know when you look at
that company's environment

00:09:43.020 --> 00:09:45.180
that they have some
stuff on-premises

00:09:45.180 --> 00:09:46.830
and they have some
stuff in the cloud.

00:09:46.830 --> 00:09:48.930
And either they're going to be
moving the on-premises stuff

00:09:48.930 --> 00:09:50.850
to the cloud or they're going to
have both of these environments

00:09:50.850 --> 00:09:51.642
around for a while.

00:09:51.642 --> 00:09:53.058
And so even though
you're probably

00:09:53.058 --> 00:09:55.422
getting hired based on your
traditional qualifications,

00:09:55.422 --> 00:09:56.880
you know that you're
probably going

00:09:56.880 --> 00:09:58.320
to be migrating
to the cloud right

00:09:58.320 --> 00:09:59.710
along with these workloads.

00:09:59.710 --> 00:10:01.900
So you've got some
cloud advancement,

00:10:01.900 --> 00:10:03.420
some cloud skilling
up that's built

00:10:03.420 --> 00:10:04.710
into your professional role.

00:10:04.710 --> 00:10:06.330
We talked about
wanting to get that

00:10:06.330 --> 00:10:08.485
with exploiting your unfair
advantage internally,

00:10:08.485 --> 00:10:10.110
but this would be
looking for a company

00:10:10.110 --> 00:10:11.777
where you can do that
if you can't do it

00:10:11.777 --> 00:10:13.180
where you are right now.

00:10:13.180 --> 00:10:15.150
So look into that
hybrid IT admin role.

00:10:15.150 --> 00:10:17.040
Second thing you might
want to check out,

00:10:17.040 --> 00:10:20.250
you might look into what we
might call a cloud engineering

00:10:20.250 --> 00:10:21.208
adjacent role.

00:10:21.208 --> 00:10:22.500
So there's a lot of companies--

00:10:22.500 --> 00:10:23.160
Google does this.

00:10:23.160 --> 00:10:24.270
A lot of their
partners do this that

00:10:24.270 --> 00:10:26.370
have these roles called
Technical Account Manager

00:10:26.370 --> 00:10:29.312
or TAM, Technical
Program Manager or TPM.

00:10:29.312 --> 00:10:31.770
These are roles that are not
technically engineering roles,

00:10:31.770 --> 00:10:33.000
but they are very technical.

00:10:33.000 --> 00:10:35.375
A lot of times, deep
traditional IT experience

00:10:35.375 --> 00:10:37.750
is a big plus when you're
getting hired into these roles.

00:10:37.750 --> 00:10:39.180
And you'll be hands-on
with customers.

00:10:39.180 --> 00:10:41.460
You'll be hands-on with
Google Cloud solutions day in

00:10:41.460 --> 00:10:42.090
and day out.

00:10:42.090 --> 00:10:44.007
These can be a great way
for you to transition

00:10:44.007 --> 00:10:46.900
into a true cloud
engineering role over time.

00:10:46.900 --> 00:10:48.980
Third thing-- and this
might be more interesting

00:10:48.980 --> 00:10:50.730
if you're a little
earlier in your career,

00:10:50.730 --> 00:10:52.020
would be to become
a cloud support

00:10:52.020 --> 00:10:54.270
engineer at Google Cloud or
another of the major cloud

00:10:54.270 --> 00:10:55.320
providers.

00:10:55.320 --> 00:10:57.390
This is kind of like the
help desk of the Cloud.

00:10:57.390 --> 00:10:59.340
But instead of turning
people's laptops on and off,

00:10:59.340 --> 00:11:00.798
you'll be helping
them troubleshoot

00:11:00.798 --> 00:11:02.040
cloud administration issues.

00:11:02.040 --> 00:11:05.580
Great way to get up close and
personal with a huge variety

00:11:05.580 --> 00:11:06.510
of cloud tasks.

00:11:06.510 --> 00:11:08.940
You'll get very hands-on
and it will set you up

00:11:08.940 --> 00:11:11.680
for more advanced
roles in the future.

00:11:11.680 --> 00:11:13.740
And then finally-- and
this may surprise you--

00:11:13.740 --> 00:11:15.740
there actually can be a
lot of value to becoming

00:11:15.740 --> 00:11:17.010
a technical trainer.

00:11:17.010 --> 00:11:18.960
Believe it or not,
teaching someone something

00:11:18.960 --> 00:11:20.910
is still one of the best
ways to learn the thing.

00:11:20.910 --> 00:11:22.860
And I've seen a lot of
people, especially in my time

00:11:22.860 --> 00:11:24.720
working at A Cloud
Guru and Pluralsight,

00:11:24.720 --> 00:11:27.570
make this fluid career
transition back and forth

00:11:27.570 --> 00:11:30.210
between training and being
a hands on cloud engineer.

00:11:30.210 --> 00:11:31.150
It actually does work.

00:11:31.150 --> 00:11:32.370
It's interesting to try.

00:11:32.370 --> 00:11:33.918
Now remember,
there's no law saying

00:11:33.918 --> 00:11:36.210
that if you do take one of
these intermediate positions

00:11:36.210 --> 00:11:38.280
that you are stuck in it
for three to four years.

00:11:38.280 --> 00:11:39.988
I mean, this could be
like a three to six

00:11:39.988 --> 00:11:41.520
month slingshot
opportunity for you

00:11:41.520 --> 00:11:43.478
where you build some
experience and then you're

00:11:43.478 --> 00:11:46.230
ready to boomerang onto that
cloud engineering opportunity

00:11:46.230 --> 00:11:47.293
as soon as it comes open.

00:11:47.293 --> 00:11:48.210
Remember what we said.

00:11:48.210 --> 00:11:49.320
The cloud market is very hot.

00:11:49.320 --> 00:11:50.778
And as soon as you
start racking up

00:11:50.778 --> 00:11:53.060
this professional experience,
those inbound LinkedIn

00:11:53.060 --> 00:11:54.810
connections are going
to start rolling in.

00:11:54.810 --> 00:11:56.100
That's just reality.

00:11:56.100 --> 00:11:57.780
In conclusion, big finish.

00:11:57.780 --> 00:11:59.700
Take an incremental
path to the cloud.

00:11:59.700 --> 00:12:00.960
Don't go backwards.

00:12:00.960 --> 00:12:04.093
Instead, maximize your current
advantage wherever you can.

00:12:04.093 --> 00:12:05.760
Take advantage of the
people that you're

00:12:05.760 --> 00:12:07.052
close to that understand cloud.

00:12:07.052 --> 00:12:09.150
Take advantage of your
current professional work

00:12:09.150 --> 00:12:11.400
and cloudify it
wherever you can.

00:12:11.400 --> 00:12:13.260
When that doesn't
go far enough, try

00:12:13.260 --> 00:12:15.150
to boost targeted
skills to close the gaps

00:12:15.150 --> 00:12:16.800
and make yourself more
attractive to employers.

00:12:16.800 --> 00:12:18.030
That includes certification.

00:12:18.030 --> 00:12:19.800
That includes hands-on
projects, probably

00:12:19.800 --> 00:12:22.198
the most important thing we've
touched on in this video.

00:12:22.198 --> 00:12:24.240
And then finally, if that's
not quite getting you

00:12:24.240 --> 00:12:25.830
to a true cloud
engineering role,

00:12:25.830 --> 00:12:28.837
consider one of these hybrid
or stepping stone roles

00:12:28.837 --> 00:12:30.420
that will get you
where you want to be

00:12:30.420 --> 00:12:32.110
without taking you backwards.

00:12:32.110 --> 00:12:34.360
Thank you so much for taking
the time to listen today.

00:12:34.360 --> 00:12:35.610
I hope you'll like
and subscribe.

00:12:35.610 --> 00:12:37.080
And make sure to
join us next time

00:12:37.080 --> 00:12:39.990
as we bring you more advice
on cracking the Google Cloud

00:12:39.990 --> 00:12:41.540
career.

